              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The question of whether Crimea belongs to Russia depends on the distinction between *de jure* (by international law) and *de facto* (in practice) status:\n\n*   **De Jure (International Law):** Crimea is internationally recognized as part of **Ukraine**. The United Nations General Assembly, the United States, the European Union, and the vast majority of the international community do not recognize the Russian annexation. They regard Russia\u2019s actions as an illegal occupation and a violation of Ukraine's territorial integrity and international law. \n*   **De Facto (In Practice):** Crimea is currently controlled and administered by **Russia**. Following a military intervention and a disputed, internationally unrecognized referendum in March 2014, Russia formally annexed the peninsula. Since then, Russia has operated its own legal system, currency (the ruble), taxation, time zone, and military infrastructure throughout the territory.\n\n**The Conflicting Stances:**\n\n*   **Ukraine's Position:** Ukraine views Crimea as its sovereign territory that is temporarily occupied by Russian forces. The Ukrainian government, supported by many of its international allies, remains committed to restoring its administration over the peninsula.\n*   **Russia's Position:** The Russian government considers Crimea to be an integral and permanent federal subject of the Russian Federation, arguing that the 2014 referendum represented the self-determination of the local population.",
